  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">STRENGTHENING LONG-TERM ELEPHANT PROTECTION AND ENHANCING HUMAN-ELEPHANT COEXISTENCE IN RUAHA-RUNGWA, TANZANIA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE RUAHA RUNGWA ECOSYSTEM IN TANZANIA LOST OVER HALF ITS ELEPHANTS BETWEEN 2009 AND 2015 DUE TO POACHING FOR THE IVORY TRADE. WHILE NO LONGER IN DECLINE THE ELEPHANT POPULATION REMAINS AT HALF OF ITS PRE POACHING CRISIS SIZE AND POPULATION RECOVERY IS THREATENED BY A RESURGENCE IN POACHING AND INCREASING HUMAN ELEPHANT CONFLICT. SOUTHERN TANZANIA ELEPHANT PROGRAM PROPOSES A FOUR YEAR PROJECT WITH THE GOAL OF STRENGTHENING LONG TERM SECURITY OF ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITATS AND ENHANCING HUMAN ELEPHANT COEXISTENCE IN THE RUAHA RUNGWA ECOSYSTEM. PROJECT OBJECTIVES ARE TO STRENGTHEN PATROL CAPACITY AND ENHANCE THE PROFESSIONALISM OF RANGERS AND VILLAGE GAME SCOUTS FACILITATE AERIAL SUPPORT AND THE USE OF CONSERVATION TECHNOLOGY FOR EARLY DETECTION OF THREATS AND STRATEGIC DEPLOYMENT OF PROTECTION RESOURCES ENHANCE PROTECTED AREA AND COMMUNITY CAPACITY FOR MITIGATING THE NEGATIVE IMPACTS OF ELEPHANTS AND DIVERSIFY COMMUNITY LIVELIHOODS AND IMPROVE HOUSEHOLD RESILIENCE THROUGH ACCESS TO SAFE AND LOW INTEREST CREDIT VIA VILLAGE SAVINGS AND LOANS ASSOCIATIONS AND BEEKEEPING. ACTIVITIES INCLUDE SKILLS TRAINING AND HUMAN RIGHTS AND SAFEGUARDING TRAINING FOR VGS AND RANGERS SUPPORT OF GROUND PATROLS IN MBOMIPA WMA IMPLEMENTING MODERN CONSERVATION TECHNOLOGIES FOR EARLY DETECTION OF THREATS AND EFFECTIVE COORDINATION AND P</narrative>
  </description>
